---
/**
 * /supported-chains: THE canonical chain list. Generated from
 * src/data/chains.ts (the same file that drives the homepage strip, the FAQ
 * answer, and the JSON-LD in Base.astro), so this page cannot drift from the
 * rest of the site. scripts/check-chain-count.mjs locks llms.txt to the same
 * list at build time.
 */
import ContentPage from '../layouts/ContentPage.astro'
import sdkPackage from '../../../../../../packages/sdk/package.json'
import { canonicalPage } from '../data/canonicalPages'
import {
  EVM_CHAINS,
  EVM_CHAIN_COUNT,
  SOVEREIGN_CHAINS,
  SOVEREIGN_CHAIN_LIST as sovereignList,
  NEAR_NETWORKS,
  NEAR_NETWORK_LIST as nearNetworkList,
} from '../data/chains'

const { updated } = canonicalPage('/supported-chains/')
// Alphabetical for the reference table (the homepage strip keeps display order).
const tableChains = [...EVM_CHAINS].sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name))

const FAQS = [
  {
    q: 'Which chains does Ophis support?',
    a: `Ophis covers ${EVM_CHAIN_COUNT} EVM chains, with ${sovereignList} as its operated sovereign networks, and supports ${nearNetworkList} as additional sources and destinations through NEAR Intents in the standalone app. External-source routes use a deposit flow, not an EVM orderbook.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'What does Ophis-operated mean?',
    a: `On ${sovereignList}, batch-auction swaps use Ophis's own settlement contracts with chain-specific fee handling. Arc uses the same capped backend improvement policy for in-market orders; see the pricing page. Resolve supported signing domains and orderbook hosts through @ophis/sdk or MCP list_chains. SDK v${sdkPackage.version} and hosted MCP include Arc (5042); do not reuse another chain's address. Bridge routes have separate costs.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'How do external-source and cross-chain swaps work?',
    a: `Select a source and destination in the same token pickers. For an additional NEAR source, review the quote and source-chain refund address, then send the specified deposit from your source wallet, including any required memo. Keep the transfer record until delivery or refund. Asset and provider availability gate every route; the list does not promise every pair in both directions. Hyperliquid means Hypercore spot balances, and Zcash requires a transparent t1/t3 address. Arc uses separate Circle routes.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Is there a testnet?',
    a: `Yes. The Sepolia testnet is available for integration testing. This page lists production chains only.`,
  },
]

const itemListLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'ItemList',
  '@id': 'https://ophis.fi/supported-chains/#chains',
  name: `EVM chains supported by Ophis`,
  numberOfItems: EVM_CHAIN_COUNT,
  itemListElement: EVM_CHAINS.map((c, i) => ({
    '@type': 'ListItem',
    position: i + 1,
    name: c.name,
  })),
}
---

<ContentPage
  title={`Supported chains: ${EVM_CHAIN_COUNT} EVM networks | Ophis`}
  description={`The canonical list of the ${EVM_CHAIN_COUNT} EVM chains Ophis supports, including the Ophis-operated sovereign networks, plus ${nearNetworkList} sources and destinations through the standalone app’s externally funded NEAR flow.`}
  path="/supported-chains/"
  heading="Supported chains"
  lede={`Ophis settles MEV-protected batch auctions on ${EVM_CHAIN_COUNT} EVM chains and supports ${nearNetworkList} through NEAR Intents, including externally funded source swaps. Availability depends on the live assets and quote.`}
  updated={updated}
  faqs={FAQS}
  extraLd={[itemListLd]}
>
  <p>
    <strong>The short version:</strong> {EVM_CHAIN_COUNT} EVM chains, {SOVEREIGN_CHAINS.length} of
    them Ophis-operated ({sovereignList}), plus {nearNetworkList} as NEAR sources and destinations in the standalone app.
    The full table with chain IDs and settlement mode:
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Chain</th>
        <th scope="col">Chain ID</th>
        <th scope="col">Settlement</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {tableChains.map((c) => (
        <tr>
          <td>{c.name}</td>
          <td><code>{c.chainId}</code></td>
          <td>
            {c.sovereign ? (
              <span class="badge">Ophis-operated</span>
            ) : (
              'Batch-auction settlement'
            )}
          </td>
        </tr>
      ))}
      {NEAR_NETWORKS.map((d) => (
        <tr>
          <td>{d}</td>
          <td>n/a</td>
          <td>NEAR source deposit or destination; route-dependent</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  {FAQS.map((f) => (
    <section>
      <h2>{f.q}</h2>
      <p>{f.a}</p>
    </section>
  ))}

  <p>
    For Arc, see <a href="/blog/swap-on-arc/">how to swap on Arc</a>.
    For Stock Token markets, see <a href="/swap/robinhood-chain/">swapping on Robinhood Chain</a>.
    Token support and executable liquidity must be checked in the live quote.
  </p>

  <h2>For agents and integrators</h2>
  <p>
    SDK v{sdkPackage.version} and the hosted MCP server cover the EVM chains above, including Arc (5042).
    External NEAR deposits are a separate app flow, not orders built by these SDK/MCP tools. Bridge coverage remains route-specific; eligible settled Arc trades count toward referral and volume-tier rebates.
  </p>
  <p>
    SDK v0.4.3 already supports Arc trading. Upgrade to v0.4.4 to include referral codes on Arc orders.
  </p>
  <p><code>npm install @ophis/sdk@{sdkPackage.version}</code></p>
  <p>
    Before every order submission, register the actual order owner so Ophis can track eligible trades for rebates.
    Registering only when the wallet connects is not sufficient. If a Safe owns the order, register the Safe address,
    not its curator's or signer's wallet.
  </p>
  <p><code>{"await enrollOphisTrader(orderOwner, { blocking: true })"}</code></p>
  <p>
    If registration fails, retry registration before submitting the order. Do not submit an already accepted order
    again just to fix registration. Registration alone does not establish partner affiliation: keep
    <code>appCode</code> set to <code>'ophis'</code> and include your valid referral code separately in the order's metadata.
    See the <a href="https://docs.ophis.fi/partners">partner integration guide</a> for the complete flow.
  </p>
  <p>
    View referral earnings and payout status in your partner dashboard.
  </p>
  <p>
    The machine-readable per-chain contract-pinning policy (settlement addresses, vault relayers,
    EIP-712 domains, orderbook hosts) ships in the
    <a href="https://ophis.fi/.well-known/agent-skills/ophis/SKILL.md">Ophis agent skill</a>
    for the chains listed in that policy. For all supported EVM chains, including Arc,
    the <a href="https://github.com/ophis-fi/ophis/tree/main/packages/sdk">@ophis/sdk</a> v{sdkPackage.version} helpers
    resolve the correct settlement domain per chain, and the
    <a href="https://docs.ophis.fi/ai-agents">MCP server</a> exposes the same data through the
    <code>list_chains</code> tool. Fee detail per chain lives on the
    <a href="/pricing/">pricing page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Arc bridges and liquidity venues</h2>
  <p>
    Arc uses USDC for gas. The app includes Circle routes where both networks and assets
    have configured mappings; consult the linked coverage guide and the live quote.
    Eligible routes are exact-input, same-wallet transfers from an EOA without smart-account
    code. Ethereum WBTC conversion follows Ethereum WBTC → Ethereum cirBTC → Arc cirBTC;
    it is not a native Bitcoin bridge or a single atomic swap.
  </p>
  <p>
    Arc routing lanes are configured for Uniswap v3, KyberSwap, Archery, Aero, Uniswap v4 and SushiSwap v3.
    A venue name identifies liquidity used by a lane; the operator can still be Ophis.
    Configuration does not guarantee a live quote or fill for every pair. See
    <a href="https://docs.ophis.fi/networks-assets">networks, assets and feature availability</a>
    for bridge recovery, confidentiality and institutional-feature boundaries.
  </p>
</ContentPage>
